var WORKFLOW_OAUTH_RE = /refusing to allow an OAuth App .+ without [`'"]?workflow[`'"]? scope/i;

function isWorkflowsPermissionRejection(stderrTail) {
   var s = String(stderrTail == null ? '' : stderrTail);
   if (!s) return false; // E4: truncated/absent tail → generic (safe default)
   return WORKFLOW_APP_RE.test(s) || WORKFLOW_OAUTH_RE.test(s);
}
``

Ticket wording: matches `refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow` and `refusing to allow an OAuth App ... without \`workflow\` scope`.

The actual GitHub messages:
- App: `refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow \`build-mobile.yml\` without \`workflows\` permission`
- OAuth: `refusing to allow an OAuth App to create or update workflow \`x.yml\` without \`workflow\` scope`
- Also PAT fine-grained: `refusing to allow a fine-grained personal access token to create or update workflow ...`? Hmm. The ticket pins exactly two shapes. I'll match the two pinned ones. Actually to be robust, maybe match `refusing to allow` + `create or update workflow`? The two pinned phrases share "refusing to allow" ... "to create or update workflow". A generic classifier: `/refusing to allow .+ to create or update workflow/i` would catch App, OAuth App, fine-grained PAT, and Deploy Key forms. But E4 says "stderr truncated before the marker phrase → treated as generic". A broad regex is more robust and still precise (this message only ever means workflow-write missing). Hmm — but the ticket says "matches `refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow` and `refusing to allow an OAuth App ... without \`workflow\` scope`". I'll implement the generic core `refusing to allow ... create or update workflow` via a single regex `refusing to allow [^`\n]*to create or update workflow` — wait, actually simpler: check both pinned variants + generic? Over-enginering. Let me follow the ticket: two regexes exactly:

1. `/refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow/` — the App form (the #1591 case). Note real message continues "workflow `file` without `workflows` permission".
2. `/refusing to allow an OAuth App .* without [`']workflow[`'] scope/` — OAuth form.

Hmm, but there's also the classic PAT form: "refusing to allow an OAuth App..." — classic PATs act as OAuth Apps. Fine-grained PAT: "refusing to allow a fine-grained personal access token to create or update workflow". Since the escalation fallback is a PAT with workflow scope, if the PAT lacks it the failure would read "refusing to allow an OAuth App ... without `workflow` scope" (classic PAT) or the fine-grained form. The ticket only pins two. I'll do the robust thing while staying within the pinned family: a single regex that matches the shared stem:

`/refusing to allow .{0,80}? to create or update workflow/i`

This matches both pinned shapes and the fine-grained PAT shape. Test fixtures: exact #1591 line + OAuth variant + unrelated rejection (REG). E4: truncated before the phrase → false. An empty tail → false.

Hmm, but a strict reading of "Classifier is a pure function isWorkflowsPermissionRejection(stderrTail) ... (matches X and Y)". The generic stem matches exactly those two families (any actor "refusing to allow" + "create or update workflow"). I'm comfortable.

Also add the distinct park reason/marker naming the missing token capability. In smAgent the park message goes through parkForkGuest(reason) → postForkParkComment. The distinct marker: a PR comment marker line, e.g.:

``
🔐 workflow-write missing — silent-update needs a workflow-capable token (gh-1106): the maintainer-edit push of main was refused because <token> cannot create or update workflow files. A workflow-write token (App with Workflows: write, or a PAT with the `workflow` scope) as `sourceToken` lifts this on the next tick. parked-head: <sha>
