Interesting: "Swap GH_TOKEN for the gh child exactly like silentUpdateBranch does, restore after (sourceToken)". So `silentUpdateBranch` (the provider) sets GH_TOKEN=silentToken, and the restore is sourceToken — implying the ambient in a tick is the source PAT, and silent operations swap IN the silent token.

Hmm — but the smAgent fork/base silent update (the git chain) doesn't set env at all; it uses ambient `${GH_TOKEN}`. The ticket states: "pushes the main merge into the fork head with silentToken (= the caller's github.token, github-actions[bot])". So at the point the update_branch rule runs, ambient GH_TOKEN IS the silent token (the factory sets GH_TOKEN=silentToken for the job or for the child). The restore contract in the ticket: "the escalation only swaps env GH_TOKEN and restores it in finally" — restore to the pre-escalation value. Since there's no get_env_variable, the known restore value is `(RUN_JOB_PARAMS || {}).silentToken` — the silent token is what the environment carries during these silent ops (per the factory wiring + the ticket's own framing: silentToken is the DEFAULT token for the silent update).

Hmm, but hold on — what if silentToken is absent from jobParams in some deployment? Then the restore would set GH_TOKEN=undefined... set_env_variable('GH_TOKEN', undefined) — risky. The existing code guards: `if (swapped && sourceTok)`. I'll do: only swap when BOTH sourceToken and silentToken exist? If silentToken is missing we can't faithfully restore... Actually we CAN: remember what we set and restore in reverse. Simplest robust approach following the codebase: 

```js
var silentTok = (RUN_JOB_PARAMS || {}).silentToken;
var sourceTok = (RUN_JOB_PARAMS || {}).sourceToken;
if (!sourceTok) → E1 park (no escalation token available)
if (DRY) → log, skip
var swapped = false;
try {
